//
// BACKLOG D4 — crew across servers, and whether they may be TRADED.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`@X164` left crew an *open maybe* — materials, salvage and
// tower-tops may cross between players and knowledge may not — and
// `@X168` settled the RESCUE half: *crew move between players, but never
// as GOODS.  You cannot buy somebody's crew; you can only be the one who
// came for them.*  What stayed open is TRADE.**
//
// ── ⚠⚠ THE SUBSTRATE FINDING, and it corrects `@X168` ────────────
//
// `@X168` closes with *"it needs **no new mechanic** — `plans/14`'s
// retrieval path is shipped, so a cross-player rescue is that path with
// a different name on the record."*
//
// ⚠⚠ **There is no record.**  A crew member has no identity at all
// apart from their position in one run's roster, and `skill.loft` says
// so in the comment that put the skills on the `Helper`: *"the roster
// slot is already this person's identity **for the whole run**"* — a
// phrase that quietly bounds it.  This file is that bound, asserted.
//
// ⚠ So BOTH halves of D4 need the same missing thing — **a person who
// exists outside a roster** — and the rescue the design calls settled
// needs it exactly as much as the trade it left open.
//
// ⚠ The contrast is worth keeping: `@X188` gave the WORLD a player key
// on its first day (`dryopea_planets/<planet>/<player>/`) *"because a
// shared world cannot be retrofitted into a path with no room for a
// name"*, and `persist.loft` stores the ground and the markers — **a RUN
// is not in it.**  The world has room for a name; the people do not.

#cwd

use dryopea;

const PALETTE: text = "examples/palette.json";

fn as_u8(n: integer) -> u8 { n as u8? ?? 0 }

// Two crew members with DIFFERENT profiles, so that anything which
// could tell them apart has something to tell apart.
fn crewed(a_build: integer, b_build: integer) -> WaveState {
    c_s = wave_state_empty();
    c_s.crew += [helper_new(1, 0)];
    c_s.crew += [helper_new(2, 0)];
    ( c_s.crew[0] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build = a_build;
    ( c_s.crew[1] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build = b_build;
    c_s
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ A crew member IS their roster slot ─────────────────────

// **The identity finding.**  Give two crew members different profiles,
// swap the slots, and every reader follows the SLOT — so `helper 0` is
// now the other person and nothing in the tree can say otherwise.
//
// ⚠ That is not a defect: `skill.loft` chose it deliberately, because
// *"two containers keyed by the same index are two facts that can
// disagree about who is who."*  ⚠⚠ What it means for D4 is that **a
// person cannot be picked out of a roster**, so neither a rescue nor a
// sale has anything to carry.
fn test_a_crew_member_is_their_roster_slot() {
    tac_s = crewed(5, 1);
    assert(( tac_s.crew[0] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build == 5,
           "slot 0 is the trained one");
    assert(( tac_s.crew[1] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build == 1,
           "slot 1 is the novice");

    // Swap the two people over.  ⚠ Nothing else in the world changes.
    ( tac_s.crew[0] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build = 1;
    ( tac_s.crew[1] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build = 5;

    assert(( tac_s.crew[0] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build == 1,
           "and after the swap `helper 0` IS the novice — the reader "
           + "follows the slot, because the slot is the identity");
    assert(( tac_s.crew[1] ?? Helper {} ).skills.build == 5,
           "and `helper 1` is the trained one");
}

// ⚠⚠ **Two crew members with the same profile are the SAME PERSON to
// every reader**, which is the absence of identity stated positively.
// `state_diff` is the sharpest witness: it is the repo's *are two runs
// in the same state?* and it cannot see a difference that is only a
// difference of WHO.
fn test_two_identical_crew_are_indistinguishable() {
    tti_a = crewed(3, 3);
    tti_b = crewed(3, 3);
    assert(state_diff(tti_a, tti_b) == "",
           "two rosters with the same profiles are the same state: "
           + "'{state_diff(tti_a, tti_b)}'");

    // ⚠ The CONTROL that says `state_diff` can see a crew difference at
    // all — otherwise the reading above is vacuous.
    tti_c = crewed(3, 4);
    assert(state_diff(tti_a, tti_c) != "",
           "CONTROL — a different PROFILE is visible: "
           + "'{state_diff(tti_a, tti_c)}'");
}

// ⚠⚠ **And what a run remembers of a person is NOTHING.**  A planet
// (BACKLOG B3) holds the GROUND and the MARKERS — `persist.loft` says a
// RUN is not in it — so a crew member does not survive the session they
// were hired into, let alone a player boundary.
//
// ⚠ This is the reading that makes D4 a question about a substrate
// rather than about a rule: there is no *person* for a rule to be about.
fn test_a_save_remembers_the_ground_and_not_the_people() {
    tas_pw = painted_empty();
    paint(tas_pw, 0, 0, as_u8(5));
    paint(tas_pw, 1, 0, as_u8(9));

    // ⚠ The save record round-trips through `MapFile`, which is capped
    // at SIX fields by the loft JSON-cast hang ([loft#876]) — the same
    // cap `damage.loft` § What the hex becomes cites for why a broken
    // wall cannot remember what was under it.  ⚠⚠ **None of the six is
    // a person**, and there is no seventh to become one.
    tas_pal = load_palette(PALETTE);
    tas_mf  = paint_to_mapfile(tas_pw, camera_default(), tas_pal, "d4");
    tas_back = mapfile_to_painted(tas_mf, tas_pal);

    assert(lookup_painted(tas_back, 0, 0) == as_u8(5),
           "the GROUND survives: {lookup_painted(tas_back, 0, 0)}");
    assert(lookup_painted(tas_back, 1, 0) == as_u8(9),
           "all of it: {lookup_painted(tas_back, 1, 0)}");
    // ⚠⚠ And that is the whole of what is remembered.  A planet
    // (BACKLOG B3) holds this pair and the MARKERS; `persist.loft` says
    // a RUN is not in it, so a crew member does not survive the session
    // they were hired into — let alone a player boundary.
}

// ─── ⚠ What the crew ARE made of, which is the other half ───────

// ⚠⚠ **A crew member's value is what their employer MADE them**
// (`@X124`'s practice loop, § P2b's *pick the profile you want, then
// give them the work*), and `@X252` makes attention the scarce
// resource.  So a market in trained crew is a market in **another
// player's attention** — which is the category `@X164` forbids for
// knowledge one axis over.
//
// ⚠ The built model already shows the shape: a level scales a rate, and
// the level is what work put there.
fn test_a_crew_members_value_is_what_work_put_there() {
    tcv_novice  = skill_factor(0);
    tcv_trained = skill_factor(5);
    assert(tcv_trained > tcv_novice,
           "practice is the whole difference between two people: "
           + "{tcv_novice} against {tcv_trained}");
    // ⚠ And it is the SAME axis a player spends on themselves — there is
    // no separate currency that could price a person.
    assert(skill_work_units(5, 100) > skill_work_units(0, 100),
           "a trained crew member converts the same seconds into more "
           + "work, which is what a buyer would be buying");
}
